<exercise label="ex-poisson-er-triage">
    <title>Emergency Room Patient Intake</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            During a busy weekend shift, trauma patients arrive at an urban emergency room triage desk according to a Poisson process with a mean rate of 80 patients per hour. If the triage nurse steps away to grab a clean stethoscope, leaving the desk unattended for exactly one minute, what is the probability that at least 1 patient arrives during that brief interval?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.2636</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the probability that <em>zero</em> patients arrive during the one-minute window (<m>P(Y=0)</m>). Remember to use the complement rule to find the probability of <m>\text{at least } 1</m> arrival.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p><m>0.7364</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! 
                    First, convert the hourly arrival rate to a 1-minute time block: <m>\lambda = \frac{80 \text{ patients}}{60 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{4}{3} \approx 1.3333</m> arrivals per minute.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Then, apply the complement rule: 
                    <me>P(Y \ge 1) = 1 - P(Y = 0) = 1 - \frac{(1.3333)^0 e^{-1.3333}}{0!} = 1 - e^{-1.3333} \approx 1 - 0.2636 = 0.7364</me>
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.3515</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the probability that <em>exactly</em> one patient arrives (<m>P(Y=1)</m>). The question requires the probability of <m>\text{at least } 1</m>, which accounts for multiple simultaneous patient walk-ins.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.5488</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. It looks like you didn't properly scale your time interval or miscalculated the exponent. Double-check your calculation for converting 80 patients per hour down to a single minute.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>
</exercise>



<exercise label="ex-poisson-campus-security">
    <title>Campus Security Patrol Allocation</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose that a campus security department devises a randomized patrol pattern to ensure unpredictability. A security vehicle may visit a high-priority quad location <m>Y = 0, 1, 2, 3, \dots</m> times per one-hour night shift block. Over a long observation window, records show each location is visited an average of once per hour. Assume that <m>Y</m> follows a Poisson probability distribution.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task 1: Identification Criteria & Lambda Identification -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-id">
        <statement>
            <p>
                How could you identify that <m>Y</m> is a Poisson random variable if that information had not been explicitly provided? 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable counts random, independent events occurring over a fixed continuum of time, where the rate is proportional to the interval size. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! The Poisson distribution models the counts of independent occurrences over a continuous interval (like an hour) given a known average rate (<m>\lambda = 1</m> visit per hour).</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable counts independent trials that can only result in binary success or failure outcomes from a fixed set of trials. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. That description fits a Binomial distribution. Poisson distributions do not have a fixed upper limit of discrete trials.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable tracks the total number of consecutive trials required until the very first success is observed. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. That description defines a Geometric random variable. Here, we are counting occurrences within a standard time block.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The visits have a uniform chance of happening at any point, meaning the count is steady. </p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Uniform distributions track spatial or temporal position variations, not accumulated integer incidence tallies. Also, \(\lambda\) is the mean rate, which is 1.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
<choice>
                <statement><p>It tracks changing success probabilities across consecutive non-replacement choices. </p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. That description applies to a Hypergeometric distribution, not a time-series event sequence.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 2: Calculate Missing Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-miss">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the probability that the campus security officer will completely miss a given high-priority location during a specific one-hour time period. In other words, compute  <m>P(Y = 0)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
        <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.368"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the Poisson probability mass function <m>P(Y = y) = \frac{\lambda^y e^{-\lambda}}{y!}</m> with parameter <m>\lambda = 1</m>:
                <me>P(Y = 0) = \frac{1^0 \cdot e^{-1}}{0!} = \frac{1 \cdot e^{-1}}{1} = e^{-1} \approx 0.368</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Task 3: Calculate More Than Once Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-more">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 What is the probability that the location will be visited more than once during a single one-hour shift block?
            </p>
               <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.264"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct!                 To find <m>P(Y \gt 1)</m>, we utilize the complement rule: 
                <me>P(Y \gt 1) = 1 - P(Y \le 1) = 1 - [P(Y = 0) + P(Y = 1)]</me>
                We already know <m>P(Y = 0) \approx 0.3679</m>. Next, calculate <m>P(Y = 1)</m>:
                <me>P(Y = 1) = \frac{1^1 \cdot e^{-1}}{1!} = e^{-1} \approx 0.3679</me>
                Combine and subtract from 1:
                <me>P(Y \gt 1) = 1 - (0.3679 + 0.3679) = 1 - 0.7358 = 0.2642</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the final probability value is <m>0.264</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
    </task>
</exercise>


